00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:27,840
But the underlying connective tissue that Flyxion exposes here is just breathtaking.

31
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:31,960
We are exploring the hidden structures of systems.

32
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,100
Like how they are built from the ground up.

33
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:48,180
Exactly. We are going to unpack how systems actually originate in the first place, how they can uncouple from physical reality to become toxic and entirely self-referential, and ultimately, how we might physically force them to anchor back to the real world.

34
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:55,020
Okay, let's unpack this. I want to start by looking at this idea of origins. Specifically, what Flyxion calls the historical origin assumption.

35
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:57,200
Right. This is where we all get tripped up.

36
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:06,800
Yeah, because by default, the way our human brains are wired, if you and I see two things that look incredibly similar, we immediately assume one of two things.

37
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:12,680
We assume either one copied the other, or they both share a direct common ancestor.

38
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:15,340
Exactly. And that assumption makes total sense, right?

39
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:15,600
Yeah.

40
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,820
It is deeply ingrained in how we are taught to categorize the world.

41
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:31,580
Oh, absolutely. If you study linguistics and you see that two different languages share an almost identical word for mother, you instantly start looking for the ancient parent language they both branched off from.

42
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:37,940
Right. Or if you are in textual criticism and you find two medieval manuscripts with the exact same grammatical error.

43
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:41,040
You assume one scribe was just peering over the shoulder of the other.

44
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:49,180
Yeah, and in evolutionary biology, if two animals share a similar bone structure in their forelimbs, we trace them back down the evolutionary tree.

45
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:53,360
Right, until we find the shared ancestor that gave them those specific bones.

46
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:58,660
Similarity is almost always viewed as the visible trace left behind by a lineage.

47
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:06,960
But Flyxion argues this default assumption is actually blinding us. It blinds us to what is really doing the work in shaping our reality.

48
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:14,300
It really does. And to prove this, they point to one of the most famous, mind-boggling biology experiments of the last century.

49
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:16,380
The Richard Lenski E. coli experiment.

50
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:19,840
Yeah. The scale of the Lenski experiment is truly something to marvel at.

51
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:27,940
Back in 1988, evolutionary biologist Richard Lenski took a single ancestral strain of E. coli bacteria.

52
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:30,920
Just one single genetic starting point.

53
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:31,620
Exactly.

54
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:39,700
He used that to found 12 identical populations. He put all 12 of these populations into the exact same environment.

55
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:41,480
Which was this liquid medium, right?

56
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:47,020
Yeah, a medium that was strictly limited in glucose, but also contained a substance called citrate.

57
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:50,760
Now, this is the crucial biological context here.

58
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:51,400
Okay, what is it?

59
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:59,280
Normally, E. coli cannot metabolize citrate in an oxygen-rich environment. It is biologically fundamentally off-limits to them.

60
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:00,020
Wow, okay.

61
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:06,980
It would be like putting a human in a room full of wood and expecting them to eat the wood for calories. We just don't have the machinery for it.

62
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:13,740
Right. So just to paint this picture for you listening, you have 12 identical glass flasks in a lab.

63
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:14,120
Yep.

64
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:21,480
You have 12 genetically identical starting populations of bacteria. And you have 12 identical chemical environments. Everything is totally controlled.

65
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:30,960
Everything is identical. And then Lenski and his team just let them reproduce, generation after generation, transferring them to fresh media every single day.

66
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:33,280
And nothing happens, right? For a very long time.

67
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:39,200
For 30,000 generations, nothing happens regarding the citrate. The bacteria are mutating, of course.

68
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:40,180
Right. Naturally.

69
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:49,180
They are evolving and adapting to become more efficient at eating the limited glucose. But they completely ignore the citrate. It remains totally inaccessible.

70
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:50,220
So it's just sitting there.

71
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:58,080
Just sitting there. But then, right around generation 31,500, something incredible happens.

72
00:05:58,200 --> 00:05:58,680
Oh, wow.

73
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:05,620
In one population, and only one out of the 12, a variant suddenly develops the ability to eat the citrate.

74
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:10,260
30,000 generations. I mean, that is a timescale that is hard to even wrap your head around.

75
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:10,940
It's massive.

76
00:06:11,060 --> 00:06:18,080
If we were talking about human generations, that would stretch back into the Pleistocene epoch, like long before Neanderthals went extinct.

77
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:24,800
It is an immense amount of time. And here is where Flyxion's core argument comes into play.

78
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:35,200
If you ask a biologist, why did that one specific population figure out how to eat citrate, while the other 11 identical populations didn't?

79
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:36,520
Right. Why just the one?

80
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:41,720
Well, the shared ancestor from 1988 gives you absolutely zero explanatory power.

81
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:43,780
Because they all share that exact same ancestor.

82
00:06:43,860 --> 00:06:44,340
Exactly.

83
00:06:44,580 --> 00:06:52,560
The historical origin is identical across the board. So looking backwards down the lineage tree explains absolutely nothing about why they diverge so radically.

84
00:06:52,560 --> 00:07:03,660
You nailed it. Flyxion draws a sharp, necessary line here between a historical origin, which is just the literal first appearance of something in linear time, and an explanatory origin.

85
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:06,460
And what exactly is the explanatory origin in this case?

86
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:11,160
It's the underlying structure of constraints that makes that appearance possible in the first place.

87
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:15,500
In the Lensky experiment, the explanatory origin isn't the ancestor.

88
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:16,120
Right.

89
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:27,940
It is the constraint space. It's the specific chemical medium, the selective pressure of starving for glucose, and crucially, the accumulated independent mutational history of that one specific population.

90
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:30,680
Okay, walk me through that mutational history part.

91
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:33,180
Because they were all mutating the whole time, right?

92
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:37,940
They were, but the genetic landscape is unfathomably huge.

93
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:48,100
Over those 30,000 generations, that one specific lineage was randomly accumulating a sequence of totally unremarkable neutral mutations.

94
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:49,860
So they didn't seem to do anything.

95
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:50,280
Right.

96
00:07:50,280 --> 00:08:02,180
These mutations didn't help them eat citrate, so they seemed useless, but what they were actually doing was quietly moving that population to a very specific, precarious location in the genetic landscape.

97
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:02,820
Oh, I see.

98
00:08:03,260 --> 00:08:11,320
They were wandering through the dark until they happened to stand on the exact square where the citrate-eating mutation was suddenly just one tiny step away.

99
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:12,400
And the others didn't.

100
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:21,220
No, the other 11 populations wandered into different parts of the genetic landscape where eating citrate was maybe 50 steps away, which is effectively impossible.

101
00:08:21,660 --> 00:08:26,760
Man, it makes me think of a maze. Imagine we have a massive, complex maze, and we drop a rat in it.

102
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:27,540
Okay, yeah.

103
00:08:27,740 --> 00:08:34,780
Eventually, the rat finds the cheese at the center. Our human instinct is to give a medal to the rat for finding the center first.

104
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:36,340
Right, we celebrate the rat.

105
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:38,180
Yeah, we celebrate the historical origin.

106
00:08:38,440 --> 00:08:38,620
Yeah.

107
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:41,540
We look at the rat and say, wow, what a genius rat.

108
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:41,900
Yeah.

109
00:08:42,020 --> 00:08:48,400
But we completely ignore the fact that the solid walls of the maze, the explanatory origin, dictated the path all along.

110
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:49,480
It's a great analogy.

111
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:53,160
The rat didn't magically intuit the location of the cheese.

112
00:08:53,340 --> 00:09:00,520
It just blindly bumped into the constraints of the maze walls until the only open path forced it toward the center.

113
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:02,860
That is a brilliant way to visualize it.

114
00:09:02,860 --> 00:09:09,060
But Flyxion refers to those maze walls mathematically as the admissibility field.

115
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:10,240
The admissibility field.

116
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:10,520
Right.

117
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:19,620
It is a field of physical, chemical, or biological constraints that makes certain outcomes not just possible, but highly probable.

118
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:20,960
So it guides the outcome.

119
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:21,480
Exactly.

120
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:24,220
The field admits certain shapes and rejects others.

121
00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:30,440
And what is so compelling about this is that it doesn't just happen in controlled laboratory settings with microscopic bacteria.

122
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:31,580
Where else does it happen?

123
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:35,200
Flyxion points to a phenomenon they call deep absence.

124
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:39,620
This is when lineage isn't just screened off like in the 12 flasks.

125
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:39,940
Okay.

126
00:09:40,180 --> 00:09:44,620
It is genuinely deeply absent, yet the exact same forms keep appearing.

127
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:47,700
Which brings us to my absolute favorite part of this deep dive.

128
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:51,540
The internet meme of everything evolving into crabs.

129
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:52,760
Carcinization.

130
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:53,360
Yes.

131
00:09:54,180 --> 00:09:54,780
Carcinization.

132
00:09:55,280 --> 00:09:58,520
If you spend any time online, you have probably seen the jokes.

133
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:00,140
Reject humanity.

134
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:01,220
Become crab.

135
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:01,900
I have seen it.

136
00:10:01,960 --> 00:10:02,140
Yeah.

