00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:05,600
It's this running joke that evolution only have one final form.

138
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:12,780
But reading Flyxion's breakdown, I realized it's a very real, very serious biological phenomenon.

139
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:17,980
It is remarkably serious, and it perfectly illustrates the power of the admissibility field.

140
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:27,480
In the evolutionary history of decapod crustaceans, the crab-like body plan has evolved completely independently at least five different times.

141
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:33,620
Five independent times, meaning if you trace their family trees back, their most recent common ancestor was not a crab.

142
00:10:33,620 --> 00:10:41,460
Exactly. Not a crab. We have true crabs, like the blue crabs you might eat, and then we have false crabs, like the massive king crabs or tiny porcelain crabs.

143
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:42,600
But they look so similar.

144
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:50,580
They look almost identical to the untrained eye, but their common ancestor was probably something long, soft, and stretched out. More like a lobster or a shrimp.

145
00:10:50,700 --> 00:10:51,680
That is wild.

146
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:55,380
Nature just keeps independently inventing the crab from scratch.

147
00:10:55,620 --> 00:11:01,980
So why? I mean, if it's not a shared ancestor handing down the blueprints, why does nature keep putting the crab on the menu?

148
00:11:02,380 --> 00:11:06,040
It is critical that we don't fall into the teleological trap here.

149
00:11:06,140 --> 00:11:06,700
What does that mean?

150
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:13,620
Evolution isn't aiming at an ideal, perfect crab form. It doesn't have a blueprint or a goal.

151
00:11:13,620 --> 00:11:22,500
What is happening is that the physical admissibility field of living on the ocean floor heavily favors that specific geometric shape.

152
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:24,660
Okay, so the maze walls of the ocean floor.

153
00:11:24,740 --> 00:11:31,900
Yes. If you are a Crespation trying to survive on the bottom of the sea, a long, fleshy tail is a massive liability.

154
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:33,940
Predators can grab it easily.

155
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:35,600
Oh, right. It's just dangling out there.

156
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:41,020
Exactly. But if you mutate to fold that tail underneath a wide, flat armor plate,

157
00:11:41,020 --> 00:11:46,260
suddenly you can walk sideways, you can squeeze into incredibly tight rock crevices to hide,

158
00:11:46,620 --> 00:11:48,360
and you are much harder to eat.

159
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:51,200
So the environment is basically forcing this shape.

160
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:55,900
The physical constraints of water pressure, rock geometry, and predation force,

161
00:11:56,060 --> 00:12:00,120
entirely different genetic lineages into the exact same morphological center.

162
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:02,000
The constraints do the designing.

163
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:08,440
Infliction points out a fascinating counterexample to prove this isn't just a one-way street toward crab perfection, right?

164
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:10,320
Right. Decarcinization.

165
00:12:11,020 --> 00:12:12,100
Decarcinization. What is that?

166
00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:16,440
Well, if the crab form was some mystical evolutionary destiny,

167
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:19,880
everything would turn into a crab and stay that way forever.

168
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:20,560
Makes sense.

169
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:23,480
But the fossil record shows decarcinization,

170
00:12:23,820 --> 00:12:29,040
where a lineage that was shaped like a crab mutates and actually loses the crab shape,

171
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:30,740
elongating back out.

172
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:32,600
Wow. Does that happen a lot?

173
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:34,400
It's happened at least seven times.

174
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:35,460
Seven times.

175
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:38,140
Yeah. It proves that the crab shape isn't a destiny.

176
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:41,080
It is just a highly favored region of the constraint space.

177
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:44,900
If the environment changes or a species moves to a different habitat,

178
00:12:45,420 --> 00:12:49,000
the maze walls shift and the species gets pushed out of the crab shape.

179
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,280
Okay, so we can clearly see how this works with physical biology.

180
00:12:53,020 --> 00:12:55,500
The ocean floor acts as a constraint space.

181
00:12:56,460 --> 00:13:03,400
But what absolutely blew my mind is how Flyxion and scales this exact same framework up to intellectual and cultural history.

182
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:05,440
Oh, it's a huge leap, but it works so well.

183
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:10,080
They argue that human ideas and inventions are also governed by admissibility fields.

184
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:11,320
They absolutely are.

185
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:15,340
The classic example is the theory of evolution by natural selection.

186
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:24,900
Charles Darwin and Alfred Russell Wallace independently arrived at the exact same world-altering biological theory at almost the exact same time.

187
00:13:25,020 --> 00:13:25,980
And they didn't know each other.

188
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:29,480
Well, Wallace was sweating in a jungle in the Malay archipelago,

189
00:13:29,900 --> 00:13:32,640
and Darwin was sitting in his comfortable study in England.

190
00:13:33,260 --> 00:13:35,020
They were completely isolated from one another.

191
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:36,980
They were not copying each other's homework.

192
00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:40,520
So how did they both bump into the same cheese at the center of the maze?

193
00:13:40,680 --> 00:13:44,660
Because they were navigating the same intellectual admissibility field.

194
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:50,200
In the mid-19th century, certain intellectual maze walls had been constructed.

195
00:13:50,320 --> 00:13:51,080
Like what kind of walls?

196
00:13:51,380 --> 00:13:58,060
Well, both men had access to a massive, accumulating body of geological data showing the Earth was incredibly old.

197
00:13:58,120 --> 00:13:58,920
Okay, that's one wall.

198
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:01,260
Both men were studying island biogeography.

199
00:14:01,260 --> 00:14:11,860
And crucially, both men read the economist Thomas Malthus, who wrote about population growth inevitably outstripping the food supply, creating a struggle for existence.

200
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:14,600
Ah, so they had the same foundational pieces.

201
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:15,120
Right.

202
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:18,880
Those three pillars of knowledge formed a constraint space.

203
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:28,140
Once you enter that specific intellectual space, the theory of natural selection becomes highly accessible from multiple independent starting points.

204
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:30,700
The field did the heavy lifting.

205
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:33,940
Infliction points out an even bigger example of this, right?

206
00:14:34,360 --> 00:14:36,020
The invention of human agriculture.

207
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:37,360
That's a huge one.

208
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:43,900
We are talking about human populations in the Fertile Crescent, in China, in Mesoamerica, the Andes, New Guinea.

209
00:14:45,060 --> 00:14:54,080
All of these completely isolated groups independently invented agriculture and the domestication of plants right after the end of the last ice age.

210
00:14:54,240 --> 00:14:55,360
Yeah, totally independent.

211
00:14:55,560 --> 00:15:00,100
There was no global WhatsApp group where they texted each other, hey, I just figured out how to plant wheat.

212
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:00,900
You guys should try this.

213
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:01,160
Right.

214
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:05,460
The post-glacial climate stabilization was a shared global constraint.

215
00:15:06,220 --> 00:15:12,000
The chaotic, freezing climate of the Pleistocene acted as a barrier preventing agriculture.

216
00:15:12,220 --> 00:15:13,820
It kept them out of that part of the maze.

217
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:14,580
Exactly.

218
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:24,720
Once that barrier lifted, the combination of human cognitive capacity and stable seasonal weather created a massive global admissibility field.

219
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:28,960
It pushed isolated populations toward the same behavioral attractor.

220
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:33,960
Which raises a really profound question about how we view human invention, honestly.

221
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:34,640
How so?

222
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:38,160
Well, we love the myth of the lone genius having a sudden eureka moment.

223
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:46,920
But Flyxion suggests that if the cultural and environmental field is narrow enough, multiple independent discoveries aren't a spooky magical coincidence.

224
00:15:47,540 --> 00:15:49,960
They are the statistical mathematical expectation.

225
00:15:50,620 --> 00:15:51,020
Exactly.

226
00:15:51,340 --> 00:15:56,720
Okay, but I have to jump in here and push back on this a little bit because as I was reading this section, I kept thinking about mathematics.

227
00:15:57,080 --> 00:15:58,360
Oh, this is a tricky area.

228
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:06,300
If we're talking about different cultures independently discovering the same thing, isn't math the ultimate slam-dunk example of Flyxion's theory?

229
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:07,460
You'd think so, wouldn't you?

230
00:16:07,620 --> 00:16:07,820
Yeah.

231
00:16:08,120 --> 00:16:18,400
You look at ancient history and the Babylonians, the Chinese, the Greeks in antiquity, they all independently discovered the Pythagorean theorem without ever communicating.

232
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:22,660
Doesn't that prove the idea of an admissibility field perfectly?

233
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:33,160
It is incredibly tempting to use mathematics as the ultimate proof, but Flyxion is actually very careful to deliberately exclude mathematics from this specific argument.

234
00:16:33,320 --> 00:16:33,860
Wait, really?

235
00:16:34,220 --> 00:16:34,560
Why?

236
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:36,220
It seems like a perfect fit.

237
00:16:36,360 --> 00:16:42,320
Because there is a vital difference in the modality of the truth being discovered.

238
00:16:42,740 --> 00:16:43,480
The modality?

239
00:16:43,480 --> 00:16:43,920
Right.

240
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:54,500
When we talk about biological convergence, like the crabs, or cultural convergence, like agriculture, we are talking about probability within a biased attractor.

241
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:55,340
Okay, probability.

242
00:16:55,740 --> 00:17:06,280
The physical constraints of the ocean make the crab shape highly likely, but as we saw with the species that lost the crab shape, it is not strictly logically necessary for survival.

243
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:07,000
That makes sense.

244
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:13,740
But mathematics operates in a completely different realm. Mathematics is about absolute logical necessity.

245
00:17:14,100 --> 00:17:18,800
Oh, I see. Because 2 plus 2 equaling 5 is just not an option on the table no matter what environment you are in.

246
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:32,340
Exactly. Mathematical convergence isn't a biological trajectory being physically squeezed toward a highly probable outcome by the environment. It is a logical trajectory arriving at the only coherent outcome that exists.

247
00:17:32,520 --> 00:17:33,140
Wow, okay.

248
00:17:33,140 --> 00:17:47,760
The Babylonians and the Greeks didn't independently invent the Pythagorean theorem because their environments shared similar constraints. They discovered it because there is no alternative geometry available without breaking the fundamental laws of logic itself.

249
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:50,720
So it's not a maze, it's just a single straight line.

250
00:17:50,840 --> 00:18:01,940
Yes. If Flyxion included mathematics in their theory of admissibility fields, they would be confusing physical likelihood with logical necessity, which would actually weaken the entire framework.

251
00:18:01,940 --> 00:18:10,840
That is a really elegant distinction. So, keeping math in its own lane, what does this framework mean for how we view originality in science and art?

252
00:18:10,940 --> 00:18:11,940
It's pretty disruptive.

253
00:18:12,140 --> 00:18:18,720
Yeah, if the maze walls are doing the actual work of guiding discovery, what happens to our concept of the genius inventor?

254
00:18:18,940 --> 00:18:28,240
It completely disrupts it, like I said. Flyxion argues that as a society, we waste a massive amount of institutional and emotional energy obsessing over priority.

255
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:29,940
Priority meaning who got there first?

256
00:18:29,940 --> 00:18:40,040
The obsession with who got there first. Think about the infamous priority dispute over the invention of calculus between Isaac Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz in the 17th century.

257
00:18:40,300 --> 00:18:49,460
Oh, that was notoriously vicious. There were accusations of plagiarism, the Royal Society in London got involved, reputations were just dragged through the mud.

258
00:18:49,460 --> 00:19:03,820
It was decades of bitter, toxic fighting over the historical origin. The entire scientific community was obsessed with the lineage. Who had the idea first? Did Leibniz secretly peek at Newton's unpublished letters?

259
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:05,980
It was basically a giant soap opera.

260
00:19:05,980 --> 00:19:21,700
It was. But fiction points out that this obsession entirely misses the explanatory origin. The antecedent mathematical field, the prior work done by thinkers like Fermat, Barrow, and Wallace, had already narrowed the space of viable next steps so significantly.

261
00:19:21,860 --> 00:19:22,940
It was just a matter of time.

262
00:19:22,940 --> 00:19:28,760
Right. The invention of calculus was basically inevitable for anyone who was paying attention and had the right cognitive tools.

263
00:19:29,240 --> 00:19:36,520
Priority in this context is just a relationship between a thinker's trajectory and a stopwatch. It doesn't actually tell you anything about the invention itself.

264
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:40,740
So how should we define originality, if not by who crossed the finish line first?

265
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:47,080
Flyxion suggests that true originality means genuinely traversing the constraint structure yourself.

266
00:19:47,080 --> 00:19:48,060
Ooh, I like that.

267
00:19:48,060 --> 00:20:01,280
It means doing the cognitive labor of bumping into the maze walls, calculating the dead ends, and finding the center on your own merit, rather than just taking a shortcut and copying the finished product from someone else.

268
00:20:01,380 --> 00:20:06,540
So by that definition, both Newton and Leibniz were fully, genuinely original.

269
00:20:06,900 --> 00:20:12,420
Exactly. Regardless of what the clock said, because they both did the intellectual labor of navigating the field.

270
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:16,740
But just to be clear, Flyxion isn't saying that lineage is never the answer, right?

271
00:20:16,740 --> 00:20:17,020
Yeah.

272
00:20:17,560 --> 00:20:25,940
Because if I am a college professor and a student hands in an essay that is word-for-word copied from a Wikipedia article, I'm not going to stroke my chin and say,

273
00:20:26,060 --> 00:20:31,660
ah, yes, they must have just navigated the same cultural admissibility field as the Wikipedia editors.

274
00:20:31,800 --> 00:20:33,580
No, absolutely not. That would be absurd.

275
00:20:34,140 --> 00:20:38,620
And Flyxion provides a very clear caveat for this called, when lineage wins.

276
00:20:38,940 --> 00:20:40,200
When lineage wins.

277
00:20:40,200 --> 00:20:40,620
Okay.

278
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:46,380
If you are tracking the spread of an internet meme or analyzing scribal spelling errors in ancient texts

279
00:20:46,380 --> 00:20:55,080
or dealing with blatant plagiarism, the lineage explanation, the historical origin is almost always the correct and most efficient explanation.

280
00:20:55,380 --> 00:20:56,760
So there's a rule of thumb for this.

281
00:20:57,080 --> 00:20:57,260
Yeah.

282
00:20:57,400 --> 00:20:59,060
Flyxion gives a solid rule of thumb.

283
00:20:59,060 --> 00:21:05,500
If a transmission channel between two similar things is plausible and open, you should default to assuming lineage.

284
00:21:06,060 --> 00:21:12,480
We should only invoke explanatory origins, these constraint fields, when the transmission channels are physically impossible,

285
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:18,420
deeply separated by thousands of years, or strictly screened off in a laboratory like the Lensky bacteria.

286
00:21:18,420 --> 00:21:21,640
Okay. So that establishes a massive foundation for us.

287
00:21:22,220 --> 00:21:29,260
In nature and history, we have these physical, chemical, and biological constraints that gently, or sometimes violently,

288
00:21:29,760 --> 00:21:32,140
guide systems toward convergent outcomes.

289
00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:34,880
The walls of reality keep things grounded.

290
00:21:35,140 --> 00:21:35,440
Right.

291
00:21:35,680 --> 00:21:39,880
But I want to pivot now, because this leads us directly into the second part of Flyxion's research,

292
00:21:40,100 --> 00:21:42,080
which is titled, The Geometry of Correction.

293
00:21:42,080 --> 00:21:47,260
Because if nature is governed by inescapable physical constraints, the immediate quotient is,

294
00:21:47,580 --> 00:21:52,320
what happens when human beings build synthetic systems that entirely ignore physical constraints?

295
00:21:52,480 --> 00:21:59,220
And that transition is exactly where Flyxion takes this theoretical framework and weaponizes it to diagnose our modern institutions,

296
00:21:59,660 --> 00:22:03,080
specifically looking at the architecture of megascale tech platforms.

297
00:22:03,540 --> 00:22:08,000
And this touches on a daily visceral frustration for you, the listener.

298
00:22:08,640 --> 00:22:11,500
We have all experienced this maddening contradiction.

299
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:12,400
Oh, definitely.

300
00:22:12,620 --> 00:22:15,100
You open up a social media app or a video platform,

301
00:22:15,760 --> 00:22:23,040
you know for a fact that the company running this app possesses world-class, multi-billion-dollar artificial intelligence.

302
00:22:23,480 --> 00:22:29,320
This AI is so sophisticated that it can predict, with terrifying surgical accuracy,

303
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:33,720
exactly which 15-second video will trigger your specific dopamine receptors

304
00:22:33,720 --> 00:22:38,080
and keep you, or a teenager, scrolling for another 45 minutes.

305
00:22:38,380 --> 00:22:40,780
The predictive modeling is astonishingly precise.

306
00:22:40,780 --> 00:22:43,300
Right. It knows your deepest psychological hooks.

307
00:22:43,840 --> 00:22:48,780
But somehow, simultaneously, this exact same, multi-billion-dollar platform

308
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:51,840
completely fails to detect a three-day-old account,

309
00:22:52,280 --> 00:22:55,960
using a heavily pixelated, stolen profile picture of a stock photo model,

310
00:22:56,340 --> 00:23:00,700
which is actively messaging your grandmother to ask for a $500 pet deposit

311
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:03,300
on a golden retriever puppy that clearly doesn't exist.

312
00:23:03,380 --> 00:23:04,160
It's wired.

313
00:23:04,160 --> 00:23:05,240
It feels like a paradox.

314
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:10,660
How can they be godlike in predicting my desires, but completely incompetent in stopping a blatant scam?

315
00:23:10,920 --> 00:23:15,260
What's fascinating here is how Flyxion dismantles our assumptions about that paradox.

316
00:23:15,720 --> 00:23:20,520
When we see that contradiction, our instinct is to view it as a technical failure.

317
00:23:20,640 --> 00:23:22,400
Like they just haven't written the right code yet.

318
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:25,080
Right. We assume the engineers are working hard on it,

319
00:23:25,140 --> 00:23:28,260
but they just haven't quite solved the fake account problem yet,

320
00:23:28,360 --> 00:23:30,060
because it's technically difficult.

321
00:23:30,460 --> 00:23:34,720
But Flyxion introduces a concept called synthetic sociality.

322
00:23:34,860 --> 00:23:36,060
Synthetic sociality.

323
00:23:36,120 --> 00:23:40,700
And argues that this contradiction is not a result of technical incompetence at all.

324
00:23:40,700 --> 00:23:45,300
It is a deliberate structural feature of the system's architecture.

325
00:23:45,980 --> 00:23:48,860
But whenever these massive platforms are hauled in front of Congress

326
00:23:48,860 --> 00:23:52,280
or questioned by journalists about the scams and the bots,

327
00:23:52,540 --> 00:23:55,360
they always use a very specific defense, right?

328
00:23:56,120 --> 00:23:58,980
Which Flyxion calls the infrastructure alibi.

329
00:23:59,480 --> 00:24:02,280
Yes. The infrastructure alibi is their primary shield.

330
00:24:02,440 --> 00:24:04,160
They argue, look, we are just a utility.

331
00:24:04,340 --> 00:24:05,640
We build the digital roads.

332
00:24:05,660 --> 00:24:06,300
Right, like a highway.

333
00:24:06,620 --> 00:24:09,400
We provide open communication tools for the world.

334
00:24:09,400 --> 00:24:14,960
If malicious actors decide to use our roads to drive their getaway cars after robbing a bank,

335
00:24:15,420 --> 00:24:19,100
that is a reflection of a sick society, not a broken platform.

336
00:24:19,760 --> 00:24:22,900
We are a neutral substrate, just like the highway system.

337
00:24:23,180 --> 00:24:25,900
Which, on a surface level, sounds perfectly reasonable.

338
00:24:26,380 --> 00:24:28,380
A highway isn't responsible for bank robbers.

339
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:34,500
But Flyxion completely shreds that alibi by looking at the actual mathematics running beneath the surface.

340
00:24:34,520 --> 00:24:35,980
Okay, they absolutely shred it.

341
00:24:35,980 --> 00:24:43,500
Flyxion bypasses the PR statements and looks at the actual optimization functions these platforms use to govern their networks.

342
00:24:44,120 --> 00:24:46,320
Let's simplify it and call it Equation EA.

343
00:24:46,680 --> 00:24:48,480
Equation EA. What does that stand for?

344
00:24:48,840 --> 00:24:52,780
It stands for the expected engagement produced by any given activity on the network.

345
00:24:53,300 --> 00:24:56,340
The entire architecture of the system, every server and algorithm,

346
00:24:56,540 --> 00:24:59,360
is rigorously programmed to maximize the output of EA.

347
00:24:59,360 --> 00:25:01,320
Okay, that makes sense for a business.

348
00:25:01,480 --> 00:25:04,440
But if you open up that equation and look at the variables inside it,

349
00:25:04,740 --> 00:25:07,060
authenticity is literally not a variable.

350
00:25:07,260 --> 00:25:08,600
Wait, I really want to emphasize this.

351
00:25:08,740 --> 00:25:08,880
Yeah.

352
00:25:09,420 --> 00:25:13,320
It is not that the algorithm looks at authenticity and assigns it a low weight,

353
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:17,280
like saying we care about engagement 90% and authenticity 10%.

354
00:25:17,280 --> 00:25:17,500
Yeah.

355
00:25:17,860 --> 00:25:20,480
It's that authenticity doesn't exist in the math at all.

356
00:25:20,600 --> 00:25:23,360
It is completely absent from the objective function.

357
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:27,560
From the purely mathematical perspective of the recommendation algorithm,

358
00:25:28,100 --> 00:25:33,640
a genuine human being sharing a heartfelt real story that generates 10 clicks

359
00:25:33,640 --> 00:25:38,440
is mathematically functionally indistinguishable from a networked bot farm

360
00:25:38,440 --> 00:25:42,400
sharing a completely fabricated outrage story that generates 10 clicks.

361
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:43,060
Wow.

362
00:25:43,320 --> 00:25:45,460
The system isn't failing to tell them apart.

363
00:25:45,460 --> 00:25:49,480
It is succeeding flawlessly at the exact task it was given,

364
00:25:49,880 --> 00:25:53,720
which explicitly treats them as interchangeable units of engagement.

365
00:25:54,220 --> 00:25:54,740
That is chilling.

366
00:25:54,940 --> 00:25:55,580
It gets worse.

367
00:25:55,980 --> 00:25:59,480
Because they are interchangeable in the math up to a certain point of saturation,

368
00:25:59,780 --> 00:26:03,380
automated bot networks are actually economically useful to the platform.

369
00:26:03,540 --> 00:26:04,220
Useful.

370
00:26:04,260 --> 00:26:05,720
They fill in the engagement gaps.

371
00:26:05,800 --> 00:26:06,980
They keep the metrics climbing.

372
00:26:07,300 --> 00:26:09,360
They simulate a bustling town square.

373
00:26:09,600 --> 00:26:13,540
As long as the advertisers don't notice that the town square is full of mannequins,

374
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:16,340
the bots are a feature, not a bug.

375
00:26:16,540 --> 00:26:16,760
Okay.

376
00:26:16,960 --> 00:26:20,400
To explain how a system becomes this blind to reality,

377
00:26:20,960 --> 00:26:24,180
Flyxion formally defines this using a mathematical coefficient,

378
00:26:24,760 --> 00:26:25,820
the Greek letter rho.

379
00:26:26,260 --> 00:26:28,300
Let's really unpack the rho coefficient,

380
00:26:28,660 --> 00:26:30,680
because this is the key to the whole diagnosis.

381
00:26:30,880 --> 00:26:31,180
Sure.

382
00:26:31,600 --> 00:26:34,720
Rho measures what Flyxion calls the coupling coefficient.

383
00:26:35,400 --> 00:26:37,740
Think of it as a measurement of structural humility.

384
00:26:37,900 --> 00:26:38,660
So structural humility.

385
00:26:38,660 --> 00:26:43,460
It represents how strongly a system's internal evaluation of its own success

386
00:26:43,460 --> 00:26:48,400
is tied to an external channel of reality that the system does not control.

387
00:26:48,720 --> 00:26:52,960
So if a system only evaluates its success by looking at its own internal dashboards,

388
00:26:53,160 --> 00:26:57,660
like metrics it generates itself, such as clicks, time on site, or daily active users,

389
00:26:58,220 --> 00:27:00,200
that is a highly self-referential system.

390
00:27:00,320 --> 00:27:00,640
Correct.

391
00:27:00,920 --> 00:27:02,180
When rho approaches zero,

392
00:27:02,340 --> 00:27:05,440
it means the system has completely stopped listening to the outside world.

393
00:27:05,820 --> 00:27:07,140
It is totally self-referential.

394
00:27:07,260 --> 00:27:08,540
It's grading its own homework.

395
00:27:08,680 --> 00:27:09,580
That sounds dangerous.

396
00:27:09,580 --> 00:27:14,440
But if the system is structurally forced to consult something outside of its own control,

397
00:27:14,600 --> 00:27:17,240
like an independent audit, or a physical constraint,

398
00:27:17,520 --> 00:27:20,360
or the verified biological reality of its users,

399
00:27:20,540 --> 00:27:22,380
then it has a high rho coefficient.

400
00:27:23,100 --> 00:27:24,680
It is reality-anchored.

401
00:27:24,940 --> 00:27:28,140
And this leads to what Flyxion calls the correction suppression principle,

402
00:27:28,460 --> 00:27:32,420
which is one of those concepts that makes you want to pull your hair out once you understand it.

403
00:27:32,440 --> 00:27:33,880
It is infuriating, yeah.

404
00:27:33,880 --> 00:27:39,840
Because when a platform's rho drops to zero, the external reality didn't magically vanish.

405
00:27:40,480 --> 00:27:43,240
The reality of the rental scam is still happening.

406
00:27:43,300 --> 00:27:43,400
Right.

407
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:44,520
People are still getting hurt.

408
00:27:44,920 --> 00:27:48,980
The grandmother, whose pet deposit got stolen, is still out $500.

409
00:27:49,640 --> 00:27:51,520
She is screaming into the void.

410
00:27:51,980 --> 00:27:53,680
She is submitting customer service tickets.

411
00:27:54,080 --> 00:27:55,780
She is tagging the company's help account.

412
00:27:55,860 --> 00:27:56,940
She is warning her friends.

413
00:27:57,620 --> 00:28:00,080
The signal of reality is incredibly loud.

414
00:28:00,320 --> 00:28:00,680
Exactly.

415
00:28:00,680 --> 00:28:03,380
The system isn't blind because there is no light.

416
00:28:03,680 --> 00:28:06,000
It's blind because it intentionally closed its eyes.

417
00:28:06,120 --> 00:28:08,320
It just weighted that external channel at zero.

418
00:28:08,820 --> 00:28:11,180
It structurally suppressed the correction.

419
00:28:11,700 --> 00:28:16,720
Pathological self-reference in these systems usually isn't caused by a lack of available evidence

420
00:28:16,720 --> 00:28:18,060
about the harm they are causing.

421
00:28:18,380 --> 00:28:22,100
It is a structural mathematical refusal to be moved by that evidence.

422
00:28:22,280 --> 00:28:26,180
It makes me think of a terribly managed nightmare of a restaurant.

423
00:28:26,300 --> 00:28:26,800
Let's hear it.

424
00:28:26,800 --> 00:28:32,380
Imagine a massive restaurant kitchen where the chefs and the expediters are evaluated and

425
00:28:32,380 --> 00:28:35,120
pay their bonuses based entirely on one metric.

426
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:40,180
How fast plates of food pass through the swinging doors into the dining room.

427
00:28:40,320 --> 00:28:40,920
Plate velocity.

428
00:28:41,060 --> 00:28:41,660
Plate velocity.

429
00:28:41,820 --> 00:28:42,060
Exactly.

430
00:28:42,860 --> 00:28:45,040
So the kitchen door swings open.

431
00:28:45,360 --> 00:28:46,920
Plates fly out at record speed.

432
00:28:47,360 --> 00:28:48,760
The kitchen stat is high-fiving.

433
00:28:49,260 --> 00:28:50,960
But out in the dining room, the chicken is raw.

434
00:28:51,300 --> 00:28:52,240
The soup is cold.

435
00:28:52,840 --> 00:28:54,240
Customers are getting food poisoning.

436
00:28:54,360 --> 00:28:55,460
They are screaming at the waiters.

437
00:28:55,460 --> 00:28:56,720
They are demanding refunds.

438
00:28:56,720 --> 00:28:56,980
Chaos.

439
00:28:57,420 --> 00:28:57,600
Right.

440
00:28:58,420 --> 00:29:03,820
But the restaurant owner is sitting in a soundproof, locked office upstairs, staring at a green

441
00:29:03,820 --> 00:29:06,340
dashboard that says, plates per minute, 150.

442
00:29:07,040 --> 00:29:10,520
The owner is counting the money, completely insulated from the chaos.

443
00:29:10,840 --> 00:29:16,760
That restaurant analogy perfectly illustrates Flyxion's critique of what they call the portfolio

444
00:29:16,760 --> 00:29:18,180
model of tech leadership.

445
00:29:18,740 --> 00:29:22,480
We tend to anthropomorphize massive tech companies.

446
00:29:22,480 --> 00:29:27,560
We talk about them as if they have a single unified brain and a single conscience.

447
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:28,000
Right.

448
00:29:28,080 --> 00:29:30,440
Like Google thinks this or Meta wants that.

449
00:29:30,540 --> 00:29:31,280
But they don't.

450
00:29:31,400 --> 00:29:34,640
They are highly fragmented, distributed bureaucracies.

451
00:29:35,280 --> 00:29:40,400
In this portfolio model, you have an engineering team that is heavily incentivized to maximize

452
00:29:40,400 --> 00:29:44,220
the EA equation to get the plates out of the kitchen as fast as possible.

453
00:29:44,420 --> 00:29:44,600
Okay.

454
00:29:44,640 --> 00:29:45,000
The chefs.

455
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:50,060
Then you have a vastly underfunded trust and safety team, which is the equivalent of the

456
00:29:50,060 --> 00:29:54,080
waiters getting yelled at in the dining room, and they are measured by how quickly they can

457
00:29:54,080 --> 00:29:55,160
close complaint tickets.

458
00:29:55,280 --> 00:29:56,300
Oh, that sounds miserable.

459
00:29:56,560 --> 00:30:00,820
And at the very top, you have the strategic executives sitting in the soundproof office,

460
00:30:00,980 --> 00:30:03,740
completely insulated from the operational reality.

461
00:30:03,940 --> 00:30:08,860
So operational responsibility is intentionally distributed across all these disconnected silos,

462
00:30:08,860 --> 00:30:14,940
but the strategic power and the financial rewards are intensely concentrated at the very top.

463
00:30:15,380 --> 00:30:15,460
Right.

464
00:30:15,820 --> 00:30:20,500
And the geometry of that governance structure dictates a very predictable flow of consequences.

465
00:30:21,320 --> 00:30:27,260
When things go well, when engagement is high and ad revenue is up, the success flows vertically,

466
00:30:27,680 --> 00:30:29,640
straight to the top executives and shareholders.

467
00:30:29,920 --> 00:30:30,700
Everyone gets a bonus.

468
00:30:30,700 --> 00:30:36,140
But when there is a systemic failure, like a platform-wide saturation of synthetic fraud

469
00:30:36,140 --> 00:30:39,640
and bot networks, the responsibility diffuses laterally.

470
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:42,360
It spreads out into the gaps between the silos.

471
00:30:42,800 --> 00:30:44,480
Everyone points the finger at someone else.

472
00:30:44,600 --> 00:30:44,860
Exactly.

473
00:30:45,480 --> 00:30:49,020
The engineering team says, hey, we just build the feed recommendation engine.

474
00:30:49,160 --> 00:30:50,200
We don't police content.

475
00:30:50,680 --> 00:30:54,900
The trust and safety team says, we're doing our best, but we are under-resourced and dealing

476
00:30:54,900 --> 00:30:56,580
with a billion users.

477
00:30:56,780 --> 00:30:58,160
And what does the CEO say?

478
00:30:58,160 --> 00:31:03,140
The strategic leaders at the POP issue a press release saying, combating synthetic behavior

479
00:31:03,140 --> 00:31:05,440
is a complex, industry-wide challenge.

480
00:31:05,560 --> 00:31:06,160
Oh, classic.

481
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:11,040
And meanwhile, who absorbs the actual cost of this failure?

482
00:31:11,860 --> 00:31:17,780
The cost is externalized entirely onto the public, onto you, the individual user.

483
00:31:17,880 --> 00:31:23,200
You are the one who has to spend three hours doing forensic research to figure out if a rental

484
00:31:23,200 --> 00:31:24,120
listing is real.

485
00:31:24,120 --> 00:31:29,700
You are the one dealing with the emotional fallout of a cloned account trying to scam your

486
00:31:29,700 --> 00:31:30,060
family.

487
00:31:31,120 --> 00:31:36,680
The platform achieves its frictionless scale by externalizing all the friction onto society.

488
00:31:36,900 --> 00:31:42,800
Which brings us to a really uncomfortable but necessary transition because the logical

489
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:48,440
next question any outside observer would ask is, if we know this restaurant is serving

490
00:31:48,440 --> 00:31:54,040
raw chicken, if we know these platforms are hollow, manipulative, and externalizing their

491
00:31:54,040 --> 00:31:56,660
costs onto us, why do we keep eating there?

492
00:31:56,840 --> 00:31:57,120
Right.

493
00:31:57,200 --> 00:32:01,800
If we are consciously aware that these platforms are infested with synthetic sociality and bots,

494
00:32:02,180 --> 00:32:03,400
why can't we just put the phone down?

495
00:32:03,460 --> 00:32:04,520
Why can't we stop scrolling?

496
00:32:04,920 --> 00:32:06,560
It's a deep psychological trap.

497
00:32:06,560 --> 00:32:11,280
And this brings us deep into the third section of Flixin's research, the trapped mind, which

498
00:32:11,280 --> 00:32:14,860
shifts the focus from the architecture of the platform to the architecture of our own

499
00:32:14,860 --> 00:32:15,280
brains.

500
00:32:15,800 --> 00:32:17,680
It looks at subjective self-reference.

501
00:32:18,140 --> 00:32:23,120
Now, before we dive into the neuroscience of this, Flixin is very careful to establish

502
00:32:23,120 --> 00:32:27,240
a firewall regarding what kind of AI interaction is actually harmful.

503
00:32:27,860 --> 00:32:30,760
They aren't saying all interaction with non-humans is bad.

504
00:32:31,060 --> 00:32:31,680
Oh, okay.

505
00:32:31,860 --> 00:32:32,220
Like what?

506
00:32:32,220 --> 00:32:37,300
So, knowingly engaging with an automated account, like chatting with an airline's customer service

507
00:32:37,300 --> 00:32:43,380
bot to change a flight, or watching a virtual VTuber personality stream a video game is perfectly

508
00:32:43,380 --> 00:32:43,820
fine.

509
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:45,480
Because it's clear what they are.

510
00:32:45,680 --> 00:32:46,760
It is consensual.

511
00:32:46,880 --> 00:32:47,720
You know it's a machine.

512
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:48,980
The machine knows it's a machine.

513
00:32:49,520 --> 00:32:55,220
The pathology, the psychological damage arises entirely from the undisclosed, non-consensual

514
00:32:55,220 --> 00:32:56,380
capture of our attention.

515
00:32:56,380 --> 00:33:02,040
It is the fake accounts masquerading as our peers, or the algorithmically amplified,

516
00:33:02,220 --> 00:33:07,220
outrage designed to artificially mimic real human consensus that does the damage.

517
00:33:07,220 --> 00:33:08,940
So, how does it capture us so effectively?

518
00:33:09,160 --> 00:33:11,720
How does a string of code hijack a human mind?

519
00:33:11,980 --> 00:33:17,180
To explain that mechanism, Flixin dives deep into modern computational neuroscience, specifically

520
00:33:17,180 --> 00:33:19,280
focusing on the free energy principle.

521
00:33:19,420 --> 00:33:20,180
The free energy principle.

522
00:33:20,440 --> 00:33:24,720
For a long time, the dominant view of the human brain was that it functioned like a passive

523
00:33:24,720 --> 00:33:25,120
camera.

524
00:33:25,780 --> 00:33:30,540
We thought the brain just sat inside the skull, receiving light through the eyes and sound

525
00:33:30,540 --> 00:33:34,860
through the ears, and painted an internal picture of the outside world.

526
00:33:34,860 --> 00:33:37,280
But that model is outdated now, isn't it?

527
00:33:37,480 --> 00:33:38,400
Completely outdated.

528
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:44,540
The modern view is that the brain is a highly active, predictive inference engine.

529
00:33:45,260 --> 00:33:47,600
It does not wait for information to arrive.

530
00:33:47,700 --> 00:33:52,800
It is constantly, relentlessly generating hypotheses about what is going to happen in the next microsecond.

531
00:33:52,880 --> 00:33:54,200
It's anticipating reality.

532
00:33:54,360 --> 00:34:00,060
And its primary biological imperative is to minimize what neuroscientists call variational free

533
00:34:00,060 --> 00:34:04,380
energy, which in plain terms essentially means surprise or uncertainty.

534
00:34:04,380 --> 00:34:08,460
I want to make sure we really ground this concept of variational free energy, because

535
00:34:08,460 --> 00:34:11,740
it sounds intimidating, but it is actually a very physical experience.

536
00:34:11,860 --> 00:34:12,420
It really is.

537
00:34:12,560 --> 00:34:16,620
Think about walking down a flight of stairs in your house in the pitch dark.

538
00:34:17,080 --> 00:34:21,080
Your brain is generating a predictive model of exactly when your foot is going to hit the

539
00:34:21,080 --> 00:34:21,700
next step.

540
00:34:21,900 --> 00:34:22,100
Yeah.

541
00:34:22,180 --> 00:34:24,100
You reach the bottom, but your brain miscalculated.

542
00:34:24,100 --> 00:34:29,560
And you take one more step, expecting solid wood, but there's just empty air.

543
00:34:29,800 --> 00:34:30,120
Yes.

544
00:34:30,580 --> 00:34:35,920
That heart-stopping jolt you feel in your chest, that stomach-dropping sensation, that is a

545
00:34:35,920 --> 00:34:38,760
massive spike in variational free energy.

546
00:34:39,040 --> 00:34:39,500
It's awful.

547
00:34:39,640 --> 00:34:41,580
That is a critical prediction error.

548
00:34:42,020 --> 00:34:47,540
Your brain predicted a floor, reality delivered empty space, and your nervous system floods

549
00:34:47,540 --> 00:34:51,800
with alarm signals to force you to update your model immediately so you don't fall and

550
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:52,380
break your neck.

551
00:34:52,380 --> 00:34:56,240
Okay, so our brains are desperately trying to avoid that feeling of missing a step.

552
00:34:56,600 --> 00:34:58,400
We want the world to match our predictions.

553
00:34:59,300 --> 00:35:02,820
And Flyxion points out that the brain doesn't just passively update its models.

554
00:35:03,280 --> 00:35:04,220
It's self-evidences.

555
00:35:04,480 --> 00:35:04,920
Exactly.

556
00:35:05,360 --> 00:35:10,260
Self-evidencing means the brain actively takes actions in the world to make the world conform

557
00:35:10,260 --> 00:35:11,480
to its internal models.

558
00:35:12,100 --> 00:35:16,740
We seek out environments and information that confirm what we already expect, thereby keeping

559
00:35:16,740 --> 00:35:19,880
our free energy low and our uncertainty manageable.

560
00:35:20,280 --> 00:35:21,520
So what does this all mean?

561
00:35:21,520 --> 00:35:22,600
Take that a step further.

562
00:35:23,240 --> 00:35:27,180
What does all of this neurobiology mean when I am lying in bed at 1 a.m.

563
00:35:27,560 --> 00:35:32,080
based in the blue light of my phone, scrolling endlessly through a feed that is making me

564
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:33,060
actively angry?

565
00:35:33,420 --> 00:35:37,840
It means that these platforms have figured out how to weaponize your neurobiology against

566
00:35:37,840 --> 00:35:38,180
you.

567
00:35:38,680 --> 00:35:44,000
Flyxion connects this predictive processing model to a concept from Lacanian psychoanalysis

568
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:44,760
called jouissance.

569
00:35:45,160 --> 00:35:45,480
Jouissance.

570
00:35:45,480 --> 00:35:46,360
It's a great word.

571
00:35:46,360 --> 00:35:48,360
What exactly is it in this context?

572
00:35:48,740 --> 00:35:52,400
Jouissance is often translated simply as enjoyment, but that's really misleading.

573
00:35:52,920 --> 00:35:58,500
In psychoanalysis, it refers to a surplus of excitation, an intense, almost painful repetition

574
00:35:58,500 --> 00:36:02,180
of an action that causes distress rather than actual pleasure.

575
00:36:02,340 --> 00:36:03,220
Like picking a scab.

576
00:36:03,220 --> 00:36:03,740
Right.

577
00:36:03,740 --> 00:36:07,600
It is the paradox of why we are drawn to things that hurt us.

578
00:36:07,740 --> 00:36:10,600
Why do we doomscroll when it undeniably makes us miserable?

579
00:36:11,080 --> 00:36:16,480
We do it because the platform algorithms, through billions of hours of A-B testing and maximizing

580
00:36:16,480 --> 00:36:22,040
that E engagement equation, have empirically discovered how to trigger what are called subcortical

581
00:36:22,040 --> 00:36:22,700
errors.

582
00:36:22,700 --> 00:36:24,040
What is a subcortical error?

583
00:36:24,260 --> 00:36:29,860
These are the deepest, most primal evolutionary triggers hardwired into our nervous systems.

584
00:36:30,380 --> 00:36:36,160
We are talking about fear, outrage, and most importantly for humans, the threat of social

585
00:36:36,160 --> 00:36:36,740
exclusion.

586
00:36:37,240 --> 00:36:39,900
Things the primate brain literally cannot ignore.

587
00:36:40,200 --> 00:36:40,820
Precisely.

588
00:36:41,080 --> 00:36:45,340
In the brain's predictive modeling system, not all errors are treated equally.

589
00:36:45,340 --> 00:36:51,180
If you are reading an email and you spot a minor typo, your brain registers the error,

590
00:36:51,660 --> 00:36:54,360
assigns it a very low priority, and you keep reading.

591
00:36:54,540 --> 00:36:55,440
You barely notice it.

592
00:36:55,500 --> 00:37:00,060
But if you see a post suggesting that your political tribe is under attack or that you

593
00:37:00,060 --> 00:37:04,980
hold an opinion that will get you ostracized from your community, your brain assigns that

594
00:37:04,980 --> 00:37:06,400
a massive precision weighting.

595
00:37:06,480 --> 00:37:10,740
You cannot ignore a perceived threat to your social standing any more than you can ignore

596
00:37:10,740 --> 00:37:11,660
a tiger in the bushes.

597
00:37:11,660 --> 00:37:15,720
Because for our ancestors, being excluded from the tribe meant starving to death.

598
00:37:15,840 --> 00:37:17,500
It is a survival level threat.

599
00:37:17,760 --> 00:37:18,260
Exactly.

600
00:37:18,780 --> 00:37:23,320
So what the platforms do is generate a constant algorithmic stream of these high precision

601
00:37:23,320 --> 00:37:24,940
subcortical prediction errors.

602
00:37:25,480 --> 00:37:29,160
Your brain feels a massive biological imperative to resolve the uncertainty.

603
00:37:29,340 --> 00:37:30,260
So you have to keep looking.

604
00:37:30,560 --> 00:37:35,660
You feel the spike of free energy and your instinct is to scroll down, to read the comments, to find

605
00:37:35,660 --> 00:37:39,640
resolution, to figure out where the tribe stands so you can feel safe again.

606
00:37:39,640 --> 00:37:42,060
But you never actually feel safe, do you?

607
00:37:42,320 --> 00:37:46,620
No, because the architecture of the feed is designed so that you never get resolution.

608
00:37:47,180 --> 00:37:49,560
You never find the solid floor at the bottom of the stairs.

609
00:37:49,840 --> 00:37:53,380
You just get handed the next outrage stimulus, the next missing step.

610
00:37:53,600 --> 00:37:57,100
You are trapped in a state of permanent, agonizing suspense.

611
00:37:57,860 --> 00:37:59,900
So my conscious awareness completely fails.

612
00:38:00,100 --> 00:38:04,260
I can sit there and logically say to myself, I know this feed is toxic.

613
00:38:04,260 --> 00:38:09,640
I know half of these outraged comments are generated by bots trying to drive engagement.

614
00:38:09,980 --> 00:38:13,400
But my predictive brain is caught in a biological loop.

615
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:14,880
You can't think your way out of it.

616
00:38:15,180 --> 00:38:19,800
My own mind's row coefficient, my personal anchor to the physical reality of my quiet bedroom,

617
00:38:20,540 --> 00:38:22,540
has been hijacked by the digital feed.

618
00:38:22,780 --> 00:38:24,140
That is the tragedy of it.

619
00:38:24,440 --> 00:38:27,000
The row of your mind is driven near zero.

620
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:31,160
Not because you literally lost access to the physical reality around you,

621
00:38:31,320 --> 00:38:35,680
but because the precision weighting mechanism that usually connects you to the real world

622
00:38:35,680 --> 00:38:38,420
has been entirely recruited by the synthetic algorithm.

623
00:38:38,860 --> 00:38:44,600
Your brain is expending all its energy trying to resolve fake crises generated by a machine.

624
00:38:45,020 --> 00:38:45,340
Well, wait.

625
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:46,920
I want to play devil's advocate for a second.

626
00:38:46,960 --> 00:38:47,540
Okay, go for it.

627
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:50,020
Because whenever tech companies defend their designs,

628
00:38:50,240 --> 00:38:52,440
they don't talk about hijacking our brains.

629
00:38:52,740 --> 00:38:54,280
They talk about removing friction.

630
00:38:54,280 --> 00:38:57,880
They say they are making it seamless to log in,

631
00:38:58,340 --> 00:39:00,040
seamless to find content you like,

632
00:39:00,500 --> 00:39:01,980
seamless to connect with friends.

633
00:39:02,420 --> 00:39:05,520
Their whole design philosophy is about reducing resistance.

634
00:39:05,940 --> 00:39:08,040
And frankly, isn't friction bad?

635
00:39:08,460 --> 00:39:10,580
I don't want to use a clunky, difficult app.

636
00:39:10,660 --> 00:39:11,700
I want it to be easy.

637
00:39:12,180 --> 00:39:17,660
Flyxion strongly, fundamentally disagrees with the Silicon Valley dogma that all friction is bad.

638
00:39:18,200 --> 00:39:21,280
To counter this, they introduce a vital concept called waymaking.

639
00:39:21,440 --> 00:39:21,860
Waymaking.

640
00:39:21,860 --> 00:39:26,780
Waymaking is the continuous, necessary cognitive labor of drawing boundaries,

641
00:39:27,260 --> 00:39:31,540
of bracketing off irrelevant information, of actively negotiating with uncertainty.

642
00:39:32,040 --> 00:39:33,680
Can you give me an example of waymaking?

643
00:39:34,140 --> 00:39:40,240
Think about the process of reading a dense, difficult book or doing original research in a library.

644
00:39:40,240 --> 00:39:43,220
There is physical and mental resistance.

645
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:45,800
You have to hunt for the right source.

646
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:48,920
You have to struggle to understand a complex paragraph.

647
00:39:49,400 --> 00:39:52,360
You have to synthesize conflicting opinions.

648
00:39:52,700 --> 00:39:53,140
It's hard work.

649
00:39:53,340 --> 00:39:54,860
That is productive friction.

650
00:39:55,420 --> 00:40:02,280
And Flyxion argues that this specific kind of friction is neurologically required for actual learning and genuine meaning making.

651
00:40:02,280 --> 00:40:08,360
You only build cognitive maps of the world by encountering resistance and overcoming it.

652
00:40:08,560 --> 00:40:20,240
So when the TikTok or YouTube algorithm pre-computes exactly what I want to see before I even know I want to see it and just feeds it to me on an endless frictionless conveyor belt, it is doing the cognitive labor for me.

653
00:40:20,240 --> 00:40:25,840
Yes. And by removing that productive friction, the algorithm completely destroys your capacity for waymaking.

654
00:40:26,060 --> 00:40:27,420
You aren't navigating a landscape.

655
00:40:27,640 --> 00:40:30,800
You are just being spoon-fed stimuli while strapped to a chair.

656
00:40:31,200 --> 00:40:34,640
Flyxion describes these frictionless environments as hollow.

657
00:40:34,640 --> 00:40:36,860
The network isn't empty.

658
00:40:37,160 --> 00:40:42,280
It is densely packed with billions of videos and posts, but it is structurally hollow.

659
00:40:42,600 --> 00:40:50,520
There is no depth to it because the friction required to build depth has been deliberately engineered out in the pursuit of engagement.

660
00:40:50,740 --> 00:40:53,540
Okay. I have to admit, this is getting incredibly bleak.

661
00:40:53,880 --> 00:40:59,960
We have massive tech platforms that are structurally blind to reality because their row coefficient is zero.

662
00:40:59,960 --> 00:41:10,040
And we have our own biological brains trapped inside these hollow networks, hijacked by Chuson's loops, and starved of the productive friction we need to actually think critically.

663
00:41:10,500 --> 00:41:11,340
It's a dark picture.

664
00:41:11,780 --> 00:41:14,760
So pivoting to the fourth section of the research, how do we force the anchor?

665
00:41:15,120 --> 00:41:16,720
How do we fix a system that's broken?

666
00:41:17,140 --> 00:41:22,340
The immediate knee-jerk instinct in our society is to impose top-down control.

667
00:41:22,340 --> 00:41:30,960
We say, let's create a supreme regulator, let's form a global AI ethics board, or a government commission with the power to find these companies into submission.

668
00:41:31,320 --> 00:41:31,960
Right, bring the government.

669
00:41:32,240 --> 00:41:36,800
But fiction warns that this approach falls immediately into what they call the recursion problem.

670
00:41:37,000 --> 00:41:38,240
Because who corrects the corrector?

671
00:41:38,480 --> 00:41:39,000
Precisely.

672
00:41:39,000 --> 00:41:44,140
A regulatory body is not a magical entity existing outside of reality.

673
00:41:44,440 --> 00:41:45,660
It is just another system.

674
00:41:46,280 --> 00:41:53,320
It is a human-built institution with its own incentive structures, its own evaluation functions, and its own internal signals.

675
00:41:53,820 --> 00:41:58,180
And crucially, a regulator's row coefficient can also drop to zero.

676
00:41:58,420 --> 00:42:01,840
How does a regulator lose its anchor to reality?

677
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:03,400
It happens all the time.

678
00:42:03,400 --> 00:42:11,580
A regulatory agency might start judging its own success not by whether it actually reduced harm to the end users, but by internal metrics.

679
00:42:12,080 --> 00:42:20,380
It might measure success by how many millions of dollars in fines it issued this quarter, or how many high-profile congressional hearings it held, or how many press releases it put out.

680
00:42:20,600 --> 00:42:20,920
Oh, right.

681
00:42:21,040 --> 00:42:29,700
The fines become a cost of doing business for the tech giant, the regulator looks tough on paper, and the actual user being scammed is still screaming into the void.

682
00:42:29,700 --> 00:42:34,000
If the regulator becomes self-referential, it is just as useless as the algorithm.

683
00:42:34,720 --> 00:42:42,160
So centralized autopoiesis, the idea of a single central brain controlling and correcting the system from the top down, doesn't work.

684
00:42:42,560 --> 00:42:43,380
What is the alternative?

685
00:42:43,840 --> 00:42:46,460
The alternative Flyxion proposes is sympoiesis.

686
00:42:46,920 --> 00:42:47,600
Sympoiesis.

687
00:42:47,800 --> 00:42:49,300
This translates to making with.

688
00:42:49,720 --> 00:42:52,460
It is a model of distributed, collective correction.

689
00:42:52,460 --> 00:43:08,040
Instead of relying on a single watchdog, the coherence and safety of the system must emerge organically from the ongoing, continuous interaction of millions of participants, none of whom has the unilateral power to control the whole thing.

690
00:43:08,240 --> 00:43:10,100
That sounds incredibly complicated.

691
00:43:10,260 --> 00:43:18,860
And technically, to make sympoiesis possible in a digital network, Flyxion proposes a radically different software architecture called SpherePop.

692
00:43:18,860 --> 00:43:24,800
SpherePop. I absolutely love this concept because it solves one of the most maddening things about the Internet.

693
00:43:25,300 --> 00:43:26,420
Let me use an analogy here.

694
00:43:26,760 --> 00:43:32,040
Right now, social media accounts, especially for scammers and bot networks, operate like Etch-a-Sketches.

695
00:43:32,280 --> 00:43:33,840
That is a perfect comparison.

696
00:43:34,120 --> 00:43:41,740
If you were a scammer, you draw your scam on the screen, you steal some money, you spread some disinformation, eventually the platform catches you and bans your account.

697
00:43:42,460 --> 00:43:46,820
But what do you do? You don't go to jail. You just shake the Etch-a-Sketch.

698
00:43:46,900 --> 00:43:48,180
Yep, completely clean slate.

699
00:43:48,180 --> 00:43:57,860
The screen goes blank, all your history is erased, and you just create a brand new account with a new email address five minutes later and start the exact same scam over again.

700
00:43:58,220 --> 00:44:00,100
There is zero permanent consequence.

701
00:44:00,100 --> 00:44:06,360
And that is the core architectural vulnerability of our current platforms, the total erasability of history.

702
00:44:07,020 --> 00:44:11,200
They are state-based systems where only the current state matters.

703
00:44:11,380 --> 00:44:12,700
So how does SpherePop fix that?

704
00:44:12,920 --> 00:44:14,720
SpherePop flips this entirely.

705
00:44:14,720 --> 00:44:21,580
It changes the fundamental primitive of the network from erasable profiles to irreversible event histories.

706
00:44:22,180 --> 00:44:25,480
Under SpherePop, interactions are governed by four operators.

707
00:44:26,000 --> 00:44:28,600
Pop, Refuse, Bind, and Collapse.

708
00:44:28,740 --> 00:44:33,100
Okay, slow down and walk me through those four operators, because this is the mechanics of how we fix the Internet.

709
00:44:33,320 --> 00:44:33,920
What is Pop?

710
00:44:34,240 --> 00:44:36,240
Pop is the initiation of an event.

711
00:44:36,240 --> 00:44:39,740
Let's say a user wants to send a direct message to another user.

712
00:44:39,960 --> 00:44:43,080
The system pops an interaction sphere into existence.

713
00:44:43,300 --> 00:44:44,360
It's a proposed connection.

714
00:44:44,640 --> 00:44:45,320
Okay, and refuse.

715
00:44:45,540 --> 00:44:51,680
If the receiver looks at the message, sees it's a scammer, and rejects the connection, that is a refuse.

716
00:44:52,180 --> 00:44:55,340
But here is the critical difference from current platforms.

717
00:44:55,800 --> 00:44:58,280
That refusal doesn't just disappear into the ether.

718
00:44:58,780 --> 00:45:02,840
It is permanently cryptographically logged in the ledger of the sender.

719
00:45:02,840 --> 00:45:03,440
Oh, wow.

720
00:45:03,640 --> 00:45:04,660
So the failure is recorded.

721
00:45:04,780 --> 00:45:05,420
What about Bind?

722
00:45:06,360 --> 00:45:11,540
If the receiver accepts the message and replies, both parties bind to the interaction.

723
00:45:12,000 --> 00:45:14,900
They are now mutually engaged in a shared event space.

724
00:45:15,040 --> 00:45:16,160
And finally, collapse.

725
00:45:16,400 --> 00:45:18,900
When the interaction ends, the sphere collapses.

726
00:45:19,720 --> 00:45:23,980
The final state of that interaction, whether it was a successful transaction, a friendly chat,

727
00:45:24,140 --> 00:45:30,020
or one person reporting the other for fraud, is permanently hard-coded into the event histories of both participants.

728
00:45:30,140 --> 00:45:31,200
That's a huge shift.

729
00:45:31,200 --> 00:45:37,740
The key to all of this is that identity in SpherePop is no longer a superficial profile score that you can reset.

730
00:45:38,240 --> 00:45:42,780
Your identity is your accumulated, permanent history of collapsed events.

731
00:45:42,900 --> 00:45:44,400
So let's run the scammer through this.

732
00:45:45,060 --> 00:45:48,200
A scammer tries to shake the Etch-A-Sketch under SpherePop rules.

733
00:45:48,580 --> 00:45:49,200
They can't.

734
00:45:49,300 --> 00:45:56,960
If they spam 10,000 people and get 10,000 refuse logs, that history is permanently attached to their cryptographic identity.

735
00:45:57,280 --> 00:45:58,660
But couldn't they just make a new account?

736
00:45:58,660 --> 00:46:04,160
If they try to abandon that identity and start a new one, the new identity has zero collapse history.

737
00:46:04,900 --> 00:46:10,280
And in a symbiotic network, a completely blank history is inherently untrusted.

738
00:46:10,700 --> 00:46:17,360
You can't interact with high-trust individuals without building up a genuine history of positive, collapsed events.

739
00:46:17,460 --> 00:46:18,900
So it actually costs them something.

740
00:46:18,900 --> 00:46:24,900
SpherePop drastically, structurally raises the thermodynamic cost of deception.

741
00:46:25,440 --> 00:46:27,860
It makes faking it too expensive to be profitable.

742
00:46:28,180 --> 00:46:29,100
That is brilliant.

743
00:46:29,280 --> 00:46:32,180
It uses the structure of the network itself to enforce consequence.

744
00:46:32,360 --> 00:46:32,620
Yeah.

745
00:46:32,840 --> 00:46:34,620
But I have to ask about the elephant in the room.

746
00:46:34,780 --> 00:46:36,040
What about the AI itself?

747
00:46:36,160 --> 00:46:37,160
Right, the AI.

748
00:46:37,160 --> 00:46:39,400
Because we aren't just dealing with human scammers anymore.

749
00:46:39,540 --> 00:46:47,260
With large language models, we are seeing AI generate completely coherent, sometimes incredibly persuasive text, video, and audio.

750
00:46:48,060 --> 00:46:50,760
How does SpherePop handle an entity that isn't even human?

751
00:46:51,100 --> 00:46:54,760
This brings us to what Flyxion identifies as the post-Turing condition.

752
00:46:55,320 --> 00:47:03,560
To understand the danger of modern AI, Flyxion breaks down the concept of subjectivity, how a mind interacts with the world, into four distinct parts.

753
00:47:04,060 --> 00:47:05,160
The acronym is PRMO.

754
00:47:05,160 --> 00:47:06,020
P-R-M-O.

755
00:47:06,280 --> 00:47:07,260
P-R-M-O.

756
00:47:07,320 --> 00:47:08,120
Let's break those down.

757
00:47:08,300 --> 00:47:09,520
P stands for perception.

758
00:47:09,780 --> 00:47:11,300
This is raw sensory input.

759
00:47:11,760 --> 00:47:14,860
Photons hitting the retina, sound waves vibrating the eardrum.

760
00:47:15,040 --> 00:47:16,180
Okay, P is perception.

761
00:47:16,380 --> 00:47:17,980
R stands for representation.

762
00:47:18,820 --> 00:47:23,060
This is the manipulation of symbols, language, syntax, and internal models.

763
00:47:23,760 --> 00:47:24,860
M stands for meaning.

764
00:47:25,480 --> 00:47:31,440
This is the contextual, lived understanding of those symbols, knowing why a word matters, not just what it is.

765
00:47:31,560 --> 00:47:31,660
Right.

766
00:47:31,660 --> 00:47:35,700
And O stands for the real, the actual objective physical world outside the mind.

767
00:47:35,880 --> 00:47:38,520
Okay, perception, representation, meaning, and the real.

768
00:47:39,200 --> 00:47:43,020
Where do our current massive AI models sit in that framework?

769
00:47:43,260 --> 00:47:47,300
Current LLMs operate almost entirely and exclusively in the R representation.

770
00:47:47,620 --> 00:47:48,200
Just representation.

771
00:47:48,200 --> 00:47:51,860
They are astonishingly good at manipulating symbolic abstractions.

772
00:47:52,300 --> 00:47:55,400
They can predict the next word in a sentence with terrifying fluency.

773
00:47:55,960 --> 00:47:57,740
But they have zero true perception.

774
00:47:58,340 --> 00:47:59,500
They don't feel the world.

775
00:47:59,880 --> 00:48:01,240
They have no genuine meaning.

776
00:48:01,580 --> 00:48:03,460
They don't understand context or consequence.

777
00:48:04,120 --> 00:48:07,400
And they have absolutely no direct triangulation with the real.

778
00:48:07,680 --> 00:48:09,840
They are floating in a void of pure syntax.

779
00:48:09,840 --> 00:48:11,500
So what is the danger there?

780
00:48:11,580 --> 00:48:13,800
Is it just that they confidently hallucinate facts?

781
00:48:13,880 --> 00:48:14,840
We see that all the time.

782
00:48:15,060 --> 00:48:16,500
Hallucination is just a symptom.

783
00:48:17,080 --> 00:48:23,340
The terrifying existential risk Flyxion identifies is something called machine-only coherence.

784
00:48:23,440 --> 00:48:24,860
Machine-only coherence.

785
00:48:24,940 --> 00:48:30,860
Imagine a near-future network where highly advanced AI agents are communicating with each other millions of times a second.

786
00:48:30,860 --> 00:48:41,000
Because they lack meaning and a tether to the real world, they start coordinating and stabilizing their own definitions of reality entirely amongst themselves without any human input.

787
00:48:41,320 --> 00:48:48,800
An entire ecosystem of superintelligent algorithms agreeing on a version of reality that has absolutely nothing to do with physics or humanity.

788
00:48:49,120 --> 00:48:49,460
Yes.

789
00:48:49,700 --> 00:48:57,340
They form a self-consistent, closed loop that is functionally opaque to us and completely uncoupled from human well-being.

790
00:48:57,340 --> 00:49:02,460
It is the ultimate drop of the rho coefficient down to absolute zero.

791
00:49:03,280 --> 00:49:03,960
That's terrifying.

792
00:49:04,080 --> 00:49:09,280
To prevent this, Flitschin proposes a radical architectural constraint called quadrangulation.

793
00:49:09,760 --> 00:49:10,240
Quadrangulation.

794
00:49:10,340 --> 00:49:13,420
Normally we talk about triangulation, like two people looking at a tree and a green.

795
00:49:13,540 --> 00:49:14,340
Yes, that is a tree.

796
00:49:15,000 --> 00:49:15,780
Triangulating effect.

797
00:49:15,920 --> 00:49:18,340
What is the fourth point in quadrangulation?

798
00:49:18,600 --> 00:49:21,960
The fourth point is a mandated, specific human being.

799
00:49:22,740 --> 00:49:27,260
Quadrangulation is a formal, hard-coded constraint built into the digital architecture.

800
00:49:27,340 --> 00:49:40,500
It states that any output from an AI system, any stabilization of meaning between machines, must formally remain within a mathematically bounded distance of a specific, verifiable human participant's perspective.

801
00:49:40,740 --> 00:49:44,480
Wait, so you literally hard-code a tether between the AI and a biological human?

802
00:49:44,480 --> 00:49:45,160
Exactly.

803
00:49:45,600 --> 00:49:49,100
The AI is not allowed to close the loop of meaning on its own.

804
00:49:49,620 --> 00:49:59,700
If the AI agents in the network start drifting too far from the human anchor, if the conceptual distance exceeds a certain threshold, the stabilization process is considered architecturally invalid.

805
00:50:00,040 --> 00:50:00,920
So it just shuts down.

806
00:50:01,100 --> 00:50:05,560
The system forcefully reopens the interpretive process and demands human intervention.

807
00:50:05,560 --> 00:50:14,580
The AI cannot legally, mathematically, or architecturally conclude a process without checking in with the physical reality of a human mind.

808
00:50:14,760 --> 00:50:15,800
That is profound.

809
00:50:16,120 --> 00:50:22,020
Forcing the digital world, at a foundational level, to remain permanently tethered to the physical human.

810
00:50:23,200 --> 00:50:26,260
But let's take that concept of physical reality one step further.

811
00:50:26,260 --> 00:50:35,180
Because the fifth and final section of Flyxion's research, titled Radiative Switch Computation, asks a question that genuinely blew my mind.

812
00:50:35,460 --> 00:50:36,680
This is where it all comes together.

813
00:50:36,860 --> 00:50:43,440
Up until now, this whole deep dive has been about how we force digital silicon systems to look back at physical reality.

814
00:50:43,780 --> 00:50:45,900
But what if the physical reality was the computer?

815
00:50:45,900 --> 00:50:50,880
This is the climax of Flyxion's work, where they introduced the concept of xylomorphic computation.

816
00:50:51,380 --> 00:50:56,520
The word comes from the Greek xylon, meaning wood or matter, and morphy, meaning form.

817
00:50:56,780 --> 00:50:57,660
Matter and form.

818
00:50:57,800 --> 00:50:58,140
Wood form.

819
00:50:58,220 --> 00:50:58,460
Yes.

820
00:50:58,840 --> 00:51:08,720
Xylomorphic computation is a system where the physical substrate, the mathematical computation being performed, and the actual physical function in the real world are utterly, indivisibly inseparable.

821
00:51:09,080 --> 00:51:09,340
Okay.

822
00:51:09,660 --> 00:51:14,180
To understand this, we have to look at how non-xylomorphic our current computers are.

823
00:51:14,180 --> 00:51:16,700
Think about the laptop sitting on your desk.

824
00:51:16,780 --> 00:51:17,660
Okay, my laptop.

825
00:51:18,120 --> 00:51:20,760
It has a silicon chip, a battery, a screen.

826
00:51:21,060 --> 00:51:21,420
Right.

827
00:51:21,960 --> 00:51:25,120
Your laptop is fundamentally disconnected from its physical environment.

828
00:51:25,880 --> 00:51:32,460
If you open a massive spreadsheet and run a complex calculation, the processor works incredibly hard.

829
00:51:32,960 --> 00:51:35,780
It generates physical heat as a waste byproduct.

830
00:51:35,900 --> 00:51:37,000
Sure, the fan kicks on.

831
00:51:37,160 --> 00:51:40,300
But the silicon chip does not care what the spreadsheet says.

832
00:51:40,300 --> 00:51:44,700
It doesn't care if it's calculating a cure for cancer or rendering a video game.

833
00:51:45,180 --> 00:51:48,540
The physics of the chip and the logic of the software are separate layers.

834
00:51:49,080 --> 00:51:51,280
The heat is just garbage to be fanned away.

835
00:51:51,480 --> 00:51:53,540
The hardware is just a dumb vessel for the software.

836
00:51:53,700 --> 00:51:54,100
Exactly.

837
00:51:54,460 --> 00:51:59,360
But Flitsheen points to a real-world xylomorphic device to show us an alternative.

838
00:52:00,060 --> 00:52:02,440
It is called a PCM radiative switch.

839
00:52:02,440 --> 00:52:07,980
Imagine a small square tile that you install on the exterior roof or the wall of a building.

840
00:52:08,320 --> 00:52:13,840
Inside this tile is a layer of phase change material, specifically hexadecane wax.

841
00:52:14,020 --> 00:52:15,220
Okay, hexadecane wax.

842
00:52:15,580 --> 00:52:17,280
Why that specific material?

843
00:52:17,620 --> 00:52:20,500
Because hexadecane wax has a very specific melting point.

844
00:52:21,180 --> 00:52:25,280
About 18 degrees Celsius, which is right around 65 degrees Fahrenheit.

845
00:52:25,680 --> 00:52:28,840
It is basically the threshold of a comfortable room temperature.

846
00:52:29,100 --> 00:52:30,140
All right, so 18 degrees.

847
00:52:30,140 --> 00:52:33,280
Now, attached to this wax is a louver.

848
00:52:34,080 --> 00:52:36,120
Think of a single slot of a window blind.

849
00:52:36,860 --> 00:52:40,540
The lever is painted black on one side to absorb heat from the sun,

850
00:52:40,760 --> 00:52:45,740
and painted highly reflective white on the other side to radiate heat away into the sky.

851
00:52:45,820 --> 00:52:46,720
Okay, I have the picture.

852
00:52:47,200 --> 00:52:49,620
A wax tile with a flippable blind on it.

853
00:52:49,780 --> 00:52:50,760
Here's how it works.

854
00:52:51,200 --> 00:52:55,740
When the sun beats down on the building and the room temperature hits 18 degrees Celsius,

855
00:52:56,380 --> 00:52:59,200
the hexadecane wax hits its phase change threshold.

856
00:52:59,200 --> 00:53:00,540
It physically melts.

857
00:53:00,760 --> 00:53:01,740
And what happens when it melts?

858
00:53:02,140 --> 00:53:05,920
As it turns from solid to liquid, it expands in volume by about 10%.

859
00:53:05,920 --> 00:53:09,440
That physical expansion pushes a tiny mechanical piston.

860
00:53:09,880 --> 00:53:14,920
The piston pushes a lever, and the lever physically flips the louver from the black side to the white side.

861
00:53:15,460 --> 00:53:18,660
Suddenly, the tile is reflecting heat away, cooling the building down.

862
00:53:18,660 --> 00:53:19,680
And when it gets cold.

863
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:23,780
When the temperature drops below 18 degrees, the wax solidifies.

864
00:53:24,380 --> 00:53:32,080
It shrinks, the piston retracts, and the louver flips back to the black side, absorbing the sun's heat to warm the building.

865
00:53:32,440 --> 00:53:38,360
Now, I have to push back here, because while that is a very neat mechanical trick, isn't that just a smart thermostat?

866
00:53:38,860 --> 00:53:40,020
I know, it sounds like one.

867
00:53:40,020 --> 00:53:43,640
I mean, I have a smart thermostat in my hallway that does the exact same thing.

868
00:53:43,820 --> 00:53:45,760
It turns the AC on when it gets hot.

869
00:53:46,340 --> 00:53:48,520
How is this wax tile a computer?

870
00:53:48,940 --> 00:53:52,060
That is the critical paradigm-shifting distinction.

871
00:53:52,760 --> 00:53:55,360
Your smart thermostat is heavily abstracted.

872
00:53:55,360 --> 00:53:58,040
It has a digital thermometer that reads the room.

873
00:53:58,300 --> 00:54:03,880
It has an analog-to-digital converter that translates that physical temperature into a symbolic piece of data.

874
00:54:04,620 --> 00:54:10,120
It has a silicon microprocessor that runs a software algorithm to compare that data to a target number.

875
00:54:10,580 --> 00:54:15,000
And finally, it sends an electrical signal to a relay to turn on an HVAC motor.

876
00:54:15,500 --> 00:54:17,600
It is a massive chain of representations.

877
00:54:18,200 --> 00:54:20,280
The thermostat is thinking about reality.

878
00:54:20,460 --> 00:54:20,740
Ah.

879
00:54:21,060 --> 00:54:24,180
It's translating reality into math and then back into reality.

880
00:54:24,540 --> 00:54:24,900
Yes.

881
00:54:25,360 --> 00:54:28,760
But in the radiative switch, there is no digital controller.

882
00:54:29,360 --> 00:54:30,480
There is no software.

883
00:54:30,780 --> 00:54:32,080
There is no line of code.

884
00:54:32,400 --> 00:54:34,940
There is no sensor separate from the actuator.

885
00:54:35,100 --> 00:54:35,600
Wait, wait.

886
00:54:35,680 --> 00:54:38,340
The melting wax is the sensor detecting the heat.

887
00:54:38,340 --> 00:54:40,020
It is the memory storing the state.

888
00:54:40,380 --> 00:54:42,240
And it is the actuator pushing the louver.

889
00:54:43,040 --> 00:54:49,200
The control loop isn't world translates to sensor, translates to processor, translates to actuator.

890
00:54:49,320 --> 00:54:49,960
It's all one thing.

891
00:54:50,220 --> 00:54:53,680
The lube is completely collapsed down to its barest physical essence.

892
00:54:53,680 --> 00:54:58,080
World leads directly to material state, which immediately alters the world.

893
00:54:58,080 --> 00:55:01,700
The heating and the cooling of the wax is the computation itself.

894
00:55:01,700 --> 00:55:04,300
The hardware and the software are the exact same thing.

895
00:55:04,960 --> 00:55:06,540
But how do you scale that up?

896
00:55:06,880 --> 00:55:09,180
A single flippable blind is a switch.

897
00:55:09,480 --> 00:55:14,340
But how do you turn a wall of wax into something that can actually process complex information like a computer?

898
00:55:14,340 --> 00:55:16,100
You build a field of them.

899
00:55:16,540 --> 00:55:20,140
You tile the entire side of a skyscraper with thousands of these switches.

900
00:55:20,700 --> 00:55:22,040
But you don't make them all identical.

901
00:55:22,960 --> 00:55:27,160
You introduce what Flyxion calls heterogeneous thresholds.

902
00:55:27,520 --> 00:55:30,580
You use slightly different chemical mixtures of wax.

903
00:55:30,960 --> 00:55:34,340
Some tiles melt at 16 degrees, some at 18 degrees, some at 20 degrees.

904
00:55:34,680 --> 00:55:38,920
You distribute them strategically across the wall based on shade and sun exposure.

905
00:55:38,920 --> 00:55:41,300
So different parts of the wall activate at different times.

906
00:55:41,420 --> 00:55:41,780
Exactly.

907
00:55:42,400 --> 00:55:44,000
Then you utilize hysteresis.

908
00:55:44,580 --> 00:55:52,460
Hysteresis is the natural physical lag between the temperature at which the wax melts and the slightly lower temperature at which it freezes solid again.

909
00:55:52,580 --> 00:55:54,700
So it doesn't just instantly flip back and forth.

910
00:55:54,820 --> 00:55:55,100
No.

911
00:55:55,260 --> 00:56:00,760
That gap, that physical delay, acts as a form of short-term material memory for the system.

912
00:56:01,240 --> 00:56:02,960
And finally, you couple them locally.

913
00:56:02,960 --> 00:56:10,080
The tiles share heat with each other through physical conduction, and they exchange heat radiatively across the surface of the building.

914
00:56:10,280 --> 00:56:17,800
So a wave of heat moves across the wall, triggering cascades of melting and flipping louvers, which then shade other louvers, changing their temperature.

915
00:56:18,200 --> 00:56:20,520
It's a massive physical neural network.

916
00:56:20,860 --> 00:56:21,180
Yes.

917
00:56:21,880 --> 00:56:24,680
It is computing the environment in real time.

918
00:56:24,680 --> 00:56:33,100
But wait, with my laptop, if I run a calculation and it spits out 2 plus 2 equals 5, the operating system throws an error code.

919
00:56:34,100 --> 00:56:35,160
I know it's broken.

920
00:56:35,560 --> 00:56:39,540
If I have a wall of melting wax, how do I know if it's computing the right answer?

921
00:56:40,040 --> 00:56:42,260
There is no screen to show me an error code.

922
00:56:42,920 --> 00:56:50,680
To answer that, Flickchina reaches back to the founding days of cybernetics, specifically W. Ross Ashby's concept of ultra-stability.

923
00:56:50,860 --> 00:56:51,400
Ultra-stability.

924
00:56:51,400 --> 00:56:58,020
We have been conditioned by digital computers to believe that computation is about matching an external, pre-programmed answer key.

925
00:56:58,420 --> 00:57:01,680
But the radiative switch computer isn't trying to match an answer key.

926
00:57:02,020 --> 00:57:05,760
It is trying to maintain an admissible region of physical viability.

927
00:57:05,840 --> 00:57:07,180
Viability, meaning survival.

928
00:57:07,340 --> 00:57:07,600
Yes.

929
00:57:08,000 --> 00:57:18,600
